{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Joule Heating.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Joule Heating.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"joule heating joule heating","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Joule Heating.md","fileName":"Joule Heating.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Joule Heating","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-15 22:37\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Thermoelectric Power Generation]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Thermoelectric Power Generation","Peltier Effect","Thomson Effect","Thermoelectric Generator","directenergyconversiontechnologies.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Joule Heating.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Joule Heating.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"joule heating joule heating","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Joule Heating.md","fileName":"Joule Heating.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Joule Heating","heading":"Joule Heating","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"Joule heating is heat produced when current flows through electrical resistance. Its rate is $Q=I^2R$, so raising current increases this loss quickly. Unlike the reversible [[Peltier Effect]] or [[Thomson Effect]], resistance heating does not change into cooling when current direction reverses. In a [[Thermoelectric Generator]], it subtracts from the temperature-difference energy available for useful output.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Thermoelectric Power Generation","Peltier Effect","Thomson Effect","Thermoelectric Generator","directenergyconversiontechnologies.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Joule Heating.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Joule Heating.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"joule heating joule heating","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Joule Heating.m